! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1258 "CHK TIME CLOCK AND INTERVAL TIMER",
! TEST 2 -- the INTERVAL TIMER half.  PDF p.7, octal
! 014034.00-014063.40, one unbroken half-word run of 48 rows,
! transcribed VERBATIM at the listing's own addresses and checked
! bit-exactly by verify_ax5b_1258_test2_listing.py.
!
! WHERE IT IS.  Routine 1258 does NOT sit in the tail of the deck
! where its number would put it: it is the FIRST listing block, PDF
! pp.5-7, immediately after the prose write-up.  These listing pages
! carry no printed page numbers, so everything here cites PDF pages.
!
! WHAT IT IS.  The deck's own write-up, PDF p.5, printed above the
! code:
!
!   ----1258---CHK TIME CLOCK AND INTERVAL TIMER.
!       -THIS TEST IS COMPOSED OF TWO ROUTINES
!       -WHICH CHECK THE FOLLOWING FUNCTIONS.
!       -TEST 1 IS A CURSORY CHECK OF
!       -THE TIME CLOCK TO SEE THAT
!       -IT STEPS CORRECTLY WITHIN
!       -THE CORRECT INTERVAL OF TIME.
!       -TEST 2 CHECKS THAT THE INTERVAL
!       -TIMER STEPS WITHIN THE
!       -ALLOTTED AMOUNT OF TIME, AND
!       -THAT EACH POSITION CAN HOLD
!       -BOTH A ONE AND A ZERO.
!
! WHERE THE CUT IS, and it is a clean one.  Test 1 (I581-I586,
! 014012.00-014033.40) is the TIME CLOCK half and occupies the whole
! of PDF p.6; it closes with its own three-row continuity block
! ending at 014033.40.  Test 2 opens the next page at 014034.00 with
! label I587 and runs to 014063.40.  Nothing straddles the boundary
! and no symbol defined in Test 1 is referenced here.  Test 1 is left
! untranscribed because this model has no time clock at all; the
! interval timer it does have a place for ($IT, word 1 bits 0-18).
!
! WHY THIS IS THE INTERVAL-TIMER ORACLE.  ax5b_16071_ts.strap already
! holds an eight-row block that sets the timer to one, spins, and asks
! whether TS came on.  That block witnesses the EXISTENCE of the
! countdown.  This one witnesses its CONTRACT, in four separate
! assertions the deck makes in its own error text:
!
!   1. THE RATE (I587/I587A/I587B).  Load bit 18 -- the value ONE --
!      into the timer, then spin a 256-count COUNT AND BRANCH watching
!      for the timer word to stop equalling 1.0.  If it never does:
!      "-INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO STEP IN / -ONE MILLISEC, TERMINATE
!      TEST 2."  One millisecond is the deck's own statement of the
!      step rate; Reference Manual printed p.34 gives 1,024 (2^10)
!      steps per second, i.e. one step per 0.9766 ms.
!
!   2. THE DECREMENT (I588).  After the step, the timer must be ZERO:
!      KV against I00Z, whose own row on PDF p.114 reads
!      "I00Z XW,0,0,0,0  -ZERO INDX WD." and prints
!      "0.00 00 000000.00 00".  The error text names both endpoints:
!      "-INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO STEP / -FROM 000000.4 TO 000000.0.
!      IX 1 / -CONTAINS ITS NEW VALUE, 0-18."  000000.4 is bit 18 set,
!      the value one; the step takes exactly one away.
!
!   3. THE RELOAD (I589/I5810).  Now load ZERO into the timer and spin
!      again waiting for it to change.  The expected new value is
!      I58K1, whose row on PDF p.5 prints "777777.40 00 000000.00 00"
!      -- bits 0-17 all ones and octal pair 40 = 100000, i.e. bit 18
!      also one: ALL ONES IN BITS 0-18 and nothing else.  The error
!      text: "-INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO STEP / -FROM 000000.0 TO
!      777777.40. IX 2 / -CONTAINS ITS NEW VALUE, 0-18."  That is the
!      1->0->all-ones reload rule, printed, from the deck itself.
!
!   4. THE BITS-19-23 CLEARING (I5811/I5812).  Load I58K2, whose p.5
!      row prints "1.77 00 000000.00 00" -- bit 17 set AND bits 18-23
!      all ones.  Bits 19-23 are OUTSIDE the timer's 0-18 field.  Spin
!      until the word changes, then compare against BIT17
!      ("1.00 00 000000.00 00", bit 17 alone).  The error text:
!      "-INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO STEP / -FROM 000001.77 TO 000001.00.
!      IX 3 / -CONTAINS ITS NEW VALUE, 0-23."
!
!      NOTE THE FIELD WIDTH IN THAT LAST LINE.  Blocks I588 and I5810
!      both say "0-18."  This one says "0-23."  The deck widens its
!      own message exactly where it is testing the five bits the
!      timer field does not cover, and the expected value has them
!      CLEAR.  That is Reference Manual printed p.34's programming
!      note -- bits 19-23 of location 1's left half are set to zero
!      when the timer is set -- stated by the deck, as the scope of
!      its own comparison, not merely implied by a constant.
!
! THE INDEX REGISTERS CORROBORATE THE COMMENT ROWS.  "IX 1", "IX 2",
! "IX 3" in the three messages are the index registers of the three
! KVs -- $X1 at 014041.00, $X2 at 014046.00, $X3 at 014053.40 -- so
! the comment-to-row assignment is checkable without reading pixel
! positions.
!
! THE SPIN IS THE SAME SHAPE THREE TIMES: LCI,$Xn,256 sets a count,
! the loop body reloads the timer word and compares, and CB,$Xn,<head>
! counts down.  Falling out of the CB means 256 iterations with no
! change, and control drops into the error report.
!
! THE BRANCH POLARITY, stated once.  A "BZ<ind>" mnemonic branches
! while the indicator is OFF and a "B<ind>" while it is ON
! (strap_asm.classify_branch_indicator: the BZ prefix sets branchOn=0).
! So BZXE -- XE is indicator 53, compare-equal -- branches while XE is
! OFF, i.e. while the two values DIFFER: the timer has moved.  BZXVZ --
! XVZ is 50, index value zero -- branches while XVZ is OFF, i.e. while
! the value is NONZERO: the timer has reloaded.  And BXE at 014054.00
! is the other polarity, branching to the exit while XE is ON, i.e.
! while the value is the expected one.
!
! THE THREE "SV,$X0,1.0" ROWS ONLY LAND DISABLED, and this range does
! not show it.  Word 1 is one of Reference Manual p.31's permanently
! protected locations, and printed p.34 names this very instruction:
! "The only store-type operations that may store in the left half of
! location 1, and hence may be used to set the interval timer, are
! STORE VALUE, STORE COUNT, STORE REFILL, and STORE VALUE IN ADDRESS,
! and these operations can be performed only when the interruption
! system is disabled.  When enabled, these operations will turn on
! indicator DS, data store, and be suppressed."  The BD that disables
! the system is outside this range -- see ax5b_16071_ts.strap, which
! makes the same point about the same word.
!
! "1.0" IS TRANSCRIBED VERBATIM and must stay that way.  opcodes.py's
! SYSTEM_SYMBOLS has "IT": (1, 0), so $IT would assemble to the same
! bits -- but the deck wrote 1.0 and this is a transcription.
!
! THE BIT TABLE GAINS THREE CONFIRMATIONS of 024522 + n, every one
! read off an encoding here and then matched against the table's own
! printing on PDF p.114: BIT1 -> 024523, BIT17 -> 024543, BIT18 ->
! 024544.
!
! ENCODING ONLY.  Running it needs SSIP's harness (SEN/SERS/SSW), the
! interruption system disabled, and an interval timer that actually
! ticks -- which this model does not have.  That is the point: this
! is the acceptance test for building one.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: none in the label names, and the "none" is a
! correction.  This file used to read the deck's leading glyph as a
! digit (1587, 15810, 100Z, 158K1, ...) and prefix an "L" to each,
! because this assembler reads a leading digit as an address.  The
! glyph was measured at 600 dpi and it is the LETTER I, which also
! makes the names legal STRAP-1 symbols unaided (C22-6531 printed
! p.10 requires an alphabetic first character), so the labels are
! written as the page prints them -- I587, I5810, I00Z, I58K1, IC258,
! ICK258 -- and nothing is prefixed.  The measurement and its scope
! are in docs/listing_typography.md.  Nothing else is changed.

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! External symbols, SYN'd at the addresses THIS listing's own printed
! encodings give.  EVERY SYN HERE IS AN INPUT TO THE CHECK, NOT A
! WITNESS: the address was read out of the very row that names the
! symbol, so that row can only re-derive what was put in.  The real
! address checks are the thirteen rows whose targets are labels
! defined INSIDE this range.
!
! Each address was obtained by reading bit 18 out of the octal pair of
! the naming row, so each is itself a checkable claim.
!
! THE ADDRESSES ARE INPUTS; THE VALUES ARE NOT.  Six of these symbols
! have their CONTENTS printed elsewhere in this same document, read
! first-hand at 600 dpi, and it is those printings -- not the names --
! that the docstring's four assertions rest on:
!   PDF p.114, "-COMMON CONSTANTS FOR FETCH ONLY":
!       I00Z  XW,0,0,0,0  -ZERO INDX WD.  ->  0.00 00 000000.00 00
!       BIT1  XW,(8)200000.00,0,0        ->  200000.00 00 000000.00 00
!       BIT17 XW,(8)1.00,0,0             ->  1.00 00 000000.00 00
!       BIT18 XW,(8).40,0,0              ->  0.40 00 000000.00 00
!   PDF p.5, routine 1258's own constant pool:
!       I58K1 XW,(8)777777.40,0,0        ->  777777.40 00 000000.00 00
!       I58K2 XW,(8)1.77,0,0             ->  1.77 00 000000.00 00
BIT18   SYN, (8)24544.0     ! from LX,$X0,BIT18     = 24544.00 10
BIT17   SYN, (8)24543.0     ! from KV,$X3,BIT17     = 24543.06 90
BIT1    SYN, (8)24523.0     ! from V+,$X13,BIT1     = 24523.32 B0
I00Z   SYN, (8)24502.0     ! from KV,$X1,I00Z      = 24502.02 90
I58K1  SYN, (8)14010.0     ! from KV,$X2,I58K1     = 14010.04 90
I58K2  SYN, (8)14011.0     ! from LX,$X0,I58K2     = 14011.00 10
IC258  SYN, (8)14006.0     ! from LX,$X13,IC258    = 14006.32 10
ICK258 SYN, (8)14007.0     ! from KV,$X13,ICK258   = 14007.32 90
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0      ! the SIC error-entry link
SENO    SYN, (8)1311.0      ! from SIC,SENO+.32     = 1311.40 80
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0      ! the error-report routine
SSW     SYN, (8)1301.0      ! the SSIP return
I60    SYN, (8)14064.0     ! from B,160            = 14064.10 00
!
! SENO's spelling is still open, but NOT for the reason this note used
! to give.  It said "this printer's letter O and digit 0 are the same
! glyph"; docs/listing_typography.md section 6 names that sentence and
! measures it wrong -- letter O runs 15-22% wider than digit 0 on both
! decks, and section 6's pairs are 55 against 45 (ax5b p.83, one
! printed comment), 49/42 and 48/43 (ax4b p.6).  What survives is
! weaker than a settled reading: n = 3
! calibration pairs, so the direction is established and the magnitude
! is within reach of ink weight and skew.  The repository already
! spells this symbol three ways (SENO here and in programs/pau_shf0.
! strap, SEN0 in programs/ax5b_160105_compare.strap, LSEN0 in
! verify_ax5b_160_test8c_listing.py).  The name is a SYN in every one
! of them, so nothing in this check or any injection depends on it.
! PDF p.114's constant pool prints the symbol's own row -- measured
! there as "SENO SYN,SEN0", a letter-O label aliased to a digit-zero
! symbol, assembling to "1311.00+ +00000000" -- which fixes the
! ADDRESS at 001311.00 and leaves this file's spelling where it was.

        SLC, (8)14034.0
!
! ---- the tick RATE: one step in one millisecond -------------------
I587   LX,  $X0, BIT18             ! -TEST 2      bit 18 = the value one
        SV,  $X0, 1.0               !              into the interval timer
        LCI, $X1, 256
I587A  KV,  $X0, 1.0               !              has the timer moved?
        BZXE, I588                 !              XE off = they differ: yes
        CB,  $X1, I587A            !              ...256 tries
I587B  SIC, SEN                    ! -INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO STEP IN
        B,   SERS                   ! -ONE MILLISEC, TERMINATE TEST 2.
        B,   I5813
!
! ---- the DECREMENT: 000000.4 -> 000000.0, exactly one -------------
I588   LX,  $X1, 1.0
        KV,  $X1, I00Z             !              the new value must be 0
        SIC, SEN                    ! -INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO STEP
        BZXE, SERS                  ! -FROM 000000.4 TO 000000.0. IX 1
                                    ! -CONTAINS ITS NEW VALUE, 0-18.
!
! ---- the RELOAD: 000000.0 -> 777777.40, all ones in bits 0-18 -----
        LX,  $X0, I00Z
        SV,  $X0, 1.0               !              timer := zero
        LCI, $X4, 256
I589   LX,  $X2, 1.0
        BZXVZ, I5810               !              XVZ off = nonzero: reloaded
        CB,  $X4, I589
        B,   I587B
I5810  KV,  $X2, I58K1            !              and it must be ALL ONES
        SIC, SEN                    ! -INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO STEP
        BZXE, SERS                  ! -FROM 000000.0 TO 777777.40. IX 2
                                    ! -CONTAINS ITS NEW VALUE, 0-18.
!
! ---- the BITS 19-23: 000001.77 -> 000001.00, the five clear -------
        LX,  $X0, I58K2            !              bit 17 + bits 18-23
        SV,  $X0, 1.0
        LCI, $X3, 256
I5811  KV,  $X0, 1.0
        BZXE, I5812
        CB,  $X3, I5811
        B,   I587B
I5812  LX,  $X3, 1.0
        KV,  $X3, BIT17             !              bit 17 ALONE
        BXE, I5813                 ! -INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO STEP
        SIC, SEN                    ! -FROM 000001.77 TO 000001.00. IX 3
        B,   SERS                   ! -CONTAINS ITS NEW VALUE, 0-23.
!
! ---- the exit, and the two continuity blocks ----------------------
I5813  B,   $+1.0
        BD,  I587
        SIC, SENO+.32
        B,   SSW
        BD,  $+.32
!
        LX,  $X13, IC258           ! -UPDATE CONTINUITY CHECK.
        V+,  $X13, BIT1             !              test 2 done = bit 1
        SX,  $X13, IC258
!
        LX,  $X13, IC258           ! -UPDATE CONTINUITY CHECK.
        KV,  $X13, ICK258          !              against the expected word
        SIC, SEN
        BZXE, SERS                  ! -CONTINUITY ERROR.
        B,   I60                   !              on to routine 160
        END, I587
